  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 309 AM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nThe aforementioned upper-level trough over the northwestern and \nwestern U.S. will gradually translate eastward into the northern\nand central Plains into Sunday night and will send a cold front\nsouthward through the Texas panhandle Sunday night into Monday\nmorning. This boundary may become oriented over the southeast New\nMexico plains and Permian Basin by Monday afternoon and evening.\nScattered showers and thunderstorms may develop along this\nboundary and POPs remain pretty decent (30-50% chance) along and \neast of the mountains late Monday afternoon into Monday \nevening/night. Model solutions remain in good agreement with\nkeeping the boundary in place over southeast New Mexico and the\nPermian Basin into Tuesday and Tuesday night. Another round of\nscattered to locally numerous showers and thunderstorms should\ndevelop along this boundary Tuesday into Tuesday night. Medium\nrange model solutions also continue to show a plume of tropical\nmoisture spreading northward from Mexico into portions of far\nsouthwest and west Texas and the southeast New Mexico plains along\nthe western periphery of the upper-level ridge Tuesday night into\nWednesday. This deeper moisture could serve to enhance convective\ncoverage over some of our western and northwestern zones,\nincluding the Davis Mountains, Van Horn Corridor, and southeast\nNew Mexico Plains Tuesday night into Wednesday, which could bring\nan increased potential for locally heavy rainfall and localized\nflash flooding. The upper-level ridge will remain oriented from\ncentral/east Texas into the Gulf Coast states into Thursday, and\nadditional moisture transport along the western periphery of the\nridge will keep good shower/storm chances in the forecast through\nlate this week over our western CWA, so we will need to continue \nto monitor for heavy rainfall/flooding potential into late week. \nThe ridge may tend to build westward toward our eastern zones late\nin the week, so storm chances may be lower from the Permian Basin\ndown into the Lower Trans Pecos Thursday and Friday. Temperatures\notherwise trend closer to normal values Tuesday-Friday, with \nhighs in the mid 80s to lower 90s and lows in the 60s for most, \nexcept 50s in the higher elevations and lower 70s along the Rio \nGrande.",
